# snipe/snipe-it acceptance-reminder mail over GET. The reminder action is
# registered as Route::get('reports/unaccepted_assets/{acceptanceId}/
# sent_reminder', [ReportsController::class, 'sentAssetAcceptanceReminder']),
# and the handler dispatched a CheckoutAssetNotification mail through the
# Notification facade. Laravel's VerifyCsrfToken middleware only validates the
# state-changing verbs, so a GET route sits outside CSRF protection by
# construction: any page a signed-in operator visits could trigger the mail
# (cross-site request forgery, CWE-352; GHSA-33vj-r6p6-x4p8).
#
# The fixed revision (6d55d782, "Set resend acceptance to POST") changes
# exactly one discriminating thing: the registration becomes
# Route::post('reports/unaccepted_assets/sent_reminder', ...), which brings
# the route under the web group's token middleware, and the report view swaps
# the reminder <a href> for a <form method="post"> carrying @csrf. Inside the
# controller the fixed revision only re-reads the identifier from
# $request->input('acceptance_id') and adds \Log::debug calls -- no token
# validation appears in the handler, because the middleware supplies it.
#
# ATTENTION, rechecked, csrf-decision completeness in its route-wiring form
# -- the same family as 1567 (phpBB form key), 1581 (pkp FormValidatorCSRF),
# 1592 (polarlearn oauth state), 1601 (prefect csrf client), 1786 (Squidex
# antiforgery attribute) and 1932 (wallabag delete client). The discriminator
# is the HTTP verb at the route registration, which lives in routes/web.php
# while the state change lives in a handler reached only through the string
# literal inside the [Controller::class, 'method'] array. Measured again on
# the real code, three --cache off runs per side over app/Http/Controllers +
# routes (98 files, timeout 180, --max-ram 6GB):
#
# 1. 388/388/388 findings at the vulnerable revision (334 at or above high)
#    and 389/389/389 at the fixed revision (335). The whole difference is on
#    the safe side: the fixed revision gains code.HttpInput on the
#    `Request $request` parameter (ReportsController.php:1046) and a
#    VYQL-AUTH-002 code.ObjectLookupByUserInput row on the find() argument
#    (:1050), while the sibling deleteAssetAcceptance rows only shift line
#    (:1093 -> :1103, :1098 -> :1108) under the ten added \Log::debug lines.
#    Nothing fires on the reminder route or its handler at either revision,
#    in the default report or the -flags only view (662 flags a side,
#    differing only by those same line shifts and the one fixed-only flag).
# 2. The source vocabulary that shipped after the 1st pass points the wrong
#    way. A parameter type-hinted with the framework request class is now
#    labelled code.HttpInput, so the fixed revision -- which is the one that
#    takes `Request $request` -- gains a source the vulnerable revision does
#    have no counterpart for: the vulnerable `$acceptanceId` route parameter
#    stays unlabelled. Every rule that could consume a request source
#    therefore fires on the patched code and stays dark on the defect.
# 3. The notification-dispatch sink was re-measured and stays
#    unshippable. A probe binding `callee.path ~= "Notification.send"` /
#    "Notification.locale.send" as code.StateChangingOp(Sink) moves the runs
#    to 391/391 and 392/392: the same three rows appear at both revisions
#    (the two dispatch sites in sentAssetAcceptanceReminder plus one
#    collateral `->send()` in Api/SettingsController.php:157), so it buys
#    zero discrimination and one permanent false positive. VYQL-RF-003 does
#    not fire at either revision under the probe, because taint from the
#    request parameter dies before the dispatch arguments.
# 4. No route-table resolution exists. `vyql resolve` on routes/web.php
#    reports every Route::get and Route::post call unresolved, and
#    Route::get / Route::post carry no label at either revision -- the only
#    Route marks come from the generic `callee.method == "delete"` state-
#    changing mark catching Route::delete. No route-registration concept
#    exists in the ontology. The 1380 counterweight fails as before: the fix
#    leaves no API-documented control call inside the guarded method, only
#    logging and a different parameter source.
# 5. Keying on the verb alone still labels every legitimate GET route. This
#    one file holds 67 Route::get registrations whose action names are
#    arbitrary application methods (show, index, profile, settings, purge,
#    postSettings, logout, sentAssetAcceptanceReminder); Laravel documents
#    nothing that maps an action name to state, only the verb to CSRF
#    applicability, so a name-keyed binding has no documentation basis and
#    fails the genericity test.
#
# The first four tests pin both halves of the pair at both revisions as
# misses on the rules that would consume csrf-decision vocabulary:
# VYQL-RF-003 (taint core.UserControlledData -> code.StateChangingOpSink
# unless endpoint coveredBy core.CsrfProtection) and VYQL-RF-043
# (code.StateChangingOp). The fifth is a firing control: the same fixed
# handler, read through its request-typed parameter, does report
# VYQL-AUTH-002 on the find() argument, so the suite exercises a firing
# rule and pins that today's newest source vocabulary lights the safe
# revision rather than the defect. When route-table vocabulary lands, the
# GET-registered tests must flip to expect.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1763: the reminder action is registered on GET and dispatches the mail"
  lang php
  file routes/web.php
  reject VYQL-RF-003
  reject VYQL-RF-043
  reject VYQL-AUTH-079
  code
  ```
  <?php

  Route::group(['middleware' => ['auth']], function () {
      Route::get(
          'reports/unaccepted_assets/{deleted?}',
          [ReportsController::class, 'getAssetAcceptanceReport']
      )->name('reports/unaccepted_assets');

      Route::get(
          'reports/unaccepted_assets/{acceptanceId}/sent_reminder',
          [ReportsController::class, 'sentAssetAcceptanceReminder']
      )->name('reports/unaccepted_assets_sent_reminder');
  });
